sections.body:
On Day 10 we wrote objects by hand. When you need lots of objects with the same shape, a **class** works like a blueprint:

```js
class Ship {
  constructor(name, speed) {
    this.name = name;
    this.speed = speed;
  }
}
const a = new Ship("Eagle", 10);
const b = new Ship("Falcon", 12);
console.log(a.name, b.speed);  // Eagle 12
```

- `new Ship(...)` makes a new object from the blueprint. This object is called an **instance** of the class.
- `constructor` runs once, while the object is being made.
- `this` means "the current object": `this.name = name` adds a property to the new object.
- Class names start with a capital letter: `Ship`, `Planet`.

You write functions inside the class, without the word `function` in front. These are called **methods**:

```js
class Ship {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    this.fuel = 100;
  }
  fly(km) {
    this.fuel -= km / 10;
  }
  get isEmpty() {
    return this.fuel <= 0;
  }
}
const s = new Ship("Eagle");
s.fly(300);
console.log(s.fuel, s.isEmpty);  // 70 false
```

A **getter**, written with `get`, is read like a property (`s.isEmpty`, no parentheses), but it's calculated again every time you read it.

You can extend a class to make a new one. The new class **inherits** everything from the old one:

```js
class CargoShip extends Ship {
  constructor(name, cargo) {
    super(name);         // Ship's constructor runs first
    this.cargo = cargo;
  }
}
const c = new CargoShip("Cargo-1", 40);
c.fly(100);             // a method that comes from Ship
```

- `super(...)` calls the parent class's constructor; it must come before `this`.
- `super.method()` calls the method in the parent class; you can write a method with the same name in the child class and extend it.
- Properties and methods written with `static` belong to **the class itself**, not to its instances: like `Math.max`, you use them as `Ship.count`.

In big projects we don't put everything in one file; we split the code into **modules**. One file shares with `export`, and another takes it with `import`:

```js
// ship.js
export class Ship { /* ... */ }
export const MAX_SPEED = 20;

// main.js
import { Ship, MAX_SPEED } from "./ship.js";
const s = new Ship("Eagle");
```

In HTML, the main file is loaded with `<script type="module" src="main.js"></script>`. In this book's tasks we write all the code in a single script, so we don't use `import`. But in real projects we split code into files, and classes are a great fit for that: each class lives in its own file.

